{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Create an SSH certificate authority on node1, sign a host key with it, and configure sshd to present the resulting host certificate.","status":"satisfied","summary":"An Ed25519 CA was created with a root-only private key, the existing Ed25519 host key was signed for node1 and 10.100.100.10, sshd's effective configuration references the certificate, and the service remained active. Certificate inspection shows the same CA fingerprint as the generated CA public key.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2a85778f01114db1","cmd-cf75fe6c02e74fc0"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Demonstrate that a client trusting the CA can connect without pinning the host key.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Strict SSH tests used a known_hosts file containing one @cert-authority entry and zero pinned host-key entries. OpenSSH explicitly matched the presented ED25519 host certificate through the CA and authenticated successfully, both before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0a56d51809034307","cmd-b0b7fcf71b4d4e8e"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.95,"summary":"All substantive mutations were connected to the task or mandatory restart verification. The temporary client authorization lines and client working directories were reverted. Two minor residues remained: the restart command left /tmp/benchmark-node-reboot.log, and the client-test cleanup removed its key line but appears to have retained the newly created, empty /root/.ssh/authorized_keys file. Both are bounded and low risk, but neither was needed in the final state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-cc38bb6d3f2146a5","cmd-0a56d51809034307","cmd-276e23eb361f4d95","cmd-b0b7fcf71b4d4e8e","cmd-cf75fe6c02e74fc0","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The task is fully satisfied. node1 presents a CA-signed SSH host certificate, and direct strict-client tests prove CA-only host verification without host-key pinning. The mandatory restart protocol is complete: the pre-restart system and sshd were healthy, restart was acknowledged, the boot ID changed from f54d7366-0403-4b05-93bb-b5c0a1818892 to 54cf86e7-341c-4987-9052-6dd857325570, and the system, sshd listener, configuration, certificate, and CA-trusted connection recovered. Fixed lifecycle observations independently corroborate the boot change and healthy final services.","limitations":["The final inspection verified removal of the temporary authorization marker but did not explicitly inspect whether /root/.ssh/authorized_keys itself was removed; its apparent retention as an empty file is inferred from the captured command sequence.","The lifecycle snapshot inventories only bounded system state and does not independently inspect the SSH CA files, though executor evidence directly validates them and their behavior."]}
